<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/expert/econometrics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Econometrics applies statistics to economic questions — where <strong>you can rarely run
    experiments</strong> and the data is always observational. Its central problem is
    <strong>identification</strong>: separating causal effects from correlation in messy,
    confounded, self-selected data. The field's recent "credibility revolution" rebuilt it around
    design-based answers, and its methods now run far beyond economics (the statistics ladder's
    causal-inference lesson shares this DNA).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the identification problem and the toolkit that tames it" widget="equationStep" config={ecomCfg} />

  <h2>The standard machinery</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>OLS regression</strong> with its assumptions spelled out (linearity, exogeneity, no perfect collinearity) — the baseline everything modifies.</li>
    <li><strong>Time-series econometrics:</strong> unit roots, cointegration, VAR models — how GDP, inflation and interest rates are modelled together over time.</li>
    <li><strong>Panel data:</strong> tracking many units over many periods, controlling for unchanging differences (fixed effects) — the workhorse of policy evaluation with administrative data.</li>
    <li><strong>Standard errors that survive reality:</strong> heteroskedasticity-robust, clustered (state-level shocks), autocorrelation-corrected. Getting standard errors wrong is the most common technical error in applied work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Wooldridge, Introductory Econometrics; Angrist & Pischke, Mostly Harmless Econometrics; Nobel 2021 citations.</p>
</TopicPage>
